{{!--
  The template cargo-about renders into pkgbuild/THIRD-PARTY-LICENSES, with
  pkgbuild/about.toml, whose header gives the command that regenerates it;
  never edit the output instead. Everything above the Licences heading is
  written here by hand, and nothing in the template prints a version, so a
  dependency bump that changes no crate and no licence text leaves the output
  as it was.

  "Notices the licence texts below do not carry" is the part that has to keep
  up with the crates. cargo-about keeps a crate's file only when the whole
  file scores as a licence text: its licence files do, and a source file does
  only when it is little more than its licence header, as some of ring's are.
  A notice in the header of any other source file never reaches the output,
  so it is written down here. bin/test's third-party notice coverage gate
  reads those headers in every crate allesbuien links, and names each
  copyright holder no Copyright line of the output names, and each BSD-style
  header whose text the output does not reproduce; add what it names here. A
  holder line keeps the shape "<crate>  Copyright ...", two spaces after the
  crate, which that gate's fixtures read.
--}}
Third-party notices for allesbuien
==================================

allesbuien is MIT-licensed: LICENSE, beside this file, is its licence. The
binary also contains code and data it does not own, and this file carries the
notices their licences ask a copy of it to travel with:

- the Rust crates listed below, linked statically, with the C, C++ and
  assembly that ring, cxx, cxx-qt and cxx-qt-lib compile into it, and the
  code that the procedural macros among them generate;
- the C++ that CXX-Qt's build tools write for it, described under CXX-Qt
  below;
- the Rust standard library, whose own notices are in COPYRIGHT-library.html
  beside this file, as the toolchain that built the binary ships them;
- code from the Qt 6 headers, described under Qt below;
- the data described under Data below.

A crate that offers a choice of licences is used under the one whose text
lists it under "Used by" below; the index of crates gives each one's own
licence expression. The Rust standard library and the crates it is built from
are used under Apache-2.0 where they offer it, and under MIT where they offer
MIT but not Apache-2.0. No versions are listed; the Cargo.lock in the
release's source archive records them.

